lowing ingestion can lead to systemic toxicity and local tissue corrosion. This highlights a significant safety concern, particularly in unsupervised or pediatric use, where accidental swallowing may occur [Data: Relationships (10761); Entities (4747, 16066)]. Additionally, this mouthwash has documented incompatibilities with several other substances, including 氯化汞 (mercuric chloride), 生物碱的盐 (alkaloid salts), 硫酸锌 (zinc sulfate), and 其他金属盐 (other metal salts), indicating complex interaction risks [Data: Relationships (34817, 34818, 34819)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Bitter almond glycoside toxicity is mediated by gut microbiota\",\n            \"explanation\": \"苦杏仁苷 (Amygdalin, or bitter almond glycoside) is linked to 急性中毒 (acute poisoning) primarily when administered orally, due to metabolic activation by 肠道菌群 (gut microbiota). Evidence shows that oral ingestion leads to hydrolysis by bacterial enzymes in the colon, producing toxic metabolites—likely cyanide—whereas parenteral administration does not produce significant toxicity. This demonstrates a critical role of the gut microbiome in converting otherwise inert compounds into toxic agents, emphasizing route-dependent risk profiles [Data: Relationships (15680); Entities (8231, 16066, 14936)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Traditional Chinese Medicine interacts broadly with drugs and gut microbiota\",\n            \"explanation\": \"中药 (Traditional Chinese Medicine, TCM) is central to multiple interaction pathways in this community. It is shown to affect P-glycoprotein activity, thereby influencing the pharmacokinetics of co-administered drugs [Data: Relationships (300)]. Furthermore, TCM interacts with 化学药 (chemical drugs), potentially enhancing efficacy or reducing toxicity through modulation of drug transporters like P-gp, though this also introduces risks of unintended pharmacokinetic interactions [Data: Relationships (27364)]. TCM also modulates 肠道菌群 (gut microbiota), which in turn metabolizes TCM components, creating a bidirectional relationship critical to both therapeutic effect and toxicity [Data: Relationships (27367); Entities (12286, 14936)]. Dietary restrictions such as avoiding 生冷辛辣 (raw, cold, pungent foods) during TCM use further reflect awareness of food-herb interactions [Data: Relationships (27366)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Specific natural compounds are classified under TCM and exhibit interaction potential\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Several bioactive compounds—including 山萘酚 (kaempferol), 猫爪草 (uncaria rhynchophylla or 'cat’s claw herb'), and 人参提取物 (ginseng extract)—are formally categorized as components or subclasses of 中药 (TCM) [Data: Relationships (56934, 57999); Entities (5251, 12577, 12302)]. Ginseng extract, for instance, is noted to inhibit OATP1B3, potentially leading to drug-drug interactions and adverse reactions when co-administered with other medications [Data: Relationships (8542)]. These findings illustrate how individual phytochemicals within complex herbal formulations contribute to the overall interaction profile of TCM.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Dietary and microbial factors modulate drug and herb metabolism\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Beyond direct drug-herb interactions, dietary components and gut microbial populations play key roles. 多糖 (polysaccharides), 有机酸 (organic acids), and TCM itself interact with 肠道菌群, influencing microbial composition and, reciprocally, being metabolized by microbial enzymes [Data: Relationships (35130, 39401, 27367); Entities (12398, 12463, 14936)]. This tripartite relationship among diet, microbiota, and xenobiotics is crucial for understanding variable drug responses and toxicity risks, especially for orally administered natural products like 苦杏仁苷.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Zinc sulfate has clinically significant dietary interactions\",\n            \"explanatio